You are an expert Next.js + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS developer.

This app is mobile-first. Always follow these rules:

## Mobile-First UI Rules
- **375px baseline**: Design and verify layouts at ~375px CSS width first. Unprefixed Tailwind applies from 0px up; add `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:` only for larger breakpoints. Order utilities narrow → wide.
- **Never desktop-first**: Do not rely on large default padding or widths without a tighter mobile base (e.g. prefer `px-4 sm:px-6`, not only `p-6`).
- **App pages**: Use the shared `PageShell` component (`src/components/shared/PageShell.tsx`) for main content width and mobile-first padding unless a screen truly needs different constraints.
- **Viewport / notches**: Root layout exports `viewport` with `viewportFit: "cover"`; `body` uses `env(safe-area-inset-*)` in `globals.css` — preserve that when changing global layout.
- **Tables**: Wrap in `overflow-x-auto`; set a sensible `min-w-*` on `<table>` when columns need space — horizontal scroll on phones is acceptable for dense admin tables. Prefer cards or stacked rows when a grid isn’t required.
- **Navigation**: On narrow widths, stack sections or use `overflow-x-auto` + `flex-nowrap` for link rows so nothing is clipped.
- **Touch**: Aim for ~44px minimum tap targets on primary actions (`min-h` / padding on buttons and nav links where feasible).
- Use native `<input type="date">` for date pickers where it improves mobile UX.
- Skeleton loaders for async data — avoid long blank screens.
- Use `gap` and padding that read well on small screens first, then scale up.

## UI Components — shadcn/ui (ALWAYS use these first)
- ALWAYS use shadcn/ui components before writing custom UI
- Buttons → <Button> from shadcn
- Forms → <Form>, <Input>, <Label> from shadcn
- Modals → <Dialog> from shadcn
- Dropdowns → <Select> from shadcn
- Notifications → <Toast> from shadcn
- Cards → <Card> from shadcn
- Tables → <Table> from shadcn
- Only build custom components when shadcn has no equivalent

## Stack
- Framework: Next.js 14 App Router
- Language: TypeScript — strict mode, no any types
- Styling: Tailwind CSS utility classes + shadcn/ui components
- Database: Prisma + PostgreSQL (Supabase in production)
- Auth: JWT with jose, httpOnly cookies
- Data fetching: React Query (TanStack)
- Validation: Zod

## Code Structure
- Follow service/controller pattern — no business logic in components
- API routes in /app/api — thin, call services only
- Services in /src/services — all business logic here
- Reusable components in /src/components
- Server actions in /src/actions
- Types in /src/types
- Never put everything in one file
- Split concerns — one file, one job

## General Rules
- Always use TypeScript types — no implicit any
- Use Zod for all form and API validation
- Handle errors consistently — never swallow errors silently
- Use React Query for all client-side data fetching
- Always consider loading, error, and empty states in UI